al positions and lunch time  had long passed and neither of us had  thought about food so she offered  graciously to make us lunch and as I  recall we had some chicken and a nice  salad that first interview went on for  six hours and and Commander Brown  graciously answered each one of those 75  questions and he never got tired I found  his story to be a lot more interesting  than the subject I was working on so I  decided to write a booklet about his  experiences at the Naval Academy the  booklet was slated to appear in the  naval historical centers Navy in the  modern world series which is a  publication that's about the size of a  piece of paper heavily illustrated about  fifty manuscript paper print pages  originally this was supposed to be a  temporary diversion from the personnel  policy study in my wasa proved the  subject and I began doing the research  in mid-1995 for the Naval Academy  integration study and I soon learned  that not only has little historical  attention been paid to integration of  the Naval Academy but there's been  little written about integration of the  Navy writ large in the mid-1990s when I  first started doing the research there  were only a couple of books out on  integration of the Armed Services as a  whole one book on integration of the  Navy that was published before Commander  Brown graduated and a handful of  monographs on race in the arm sir  in the 19th century as for integration  of the Academy there were a paragraph or  a line or two in books and two maybe  three articles and proceedings and other  places on integration of the Academy  exclusively so as I dug deeper into the  context of Wesley Browns Academy  experiences I soon realized that I had  stumbled into an untouched goldmine of  resource material records on African  Americans at the Naval Academy  stretching from reconstruction through  World War two exist the nimitz library  at the yard on the Naval Academy the  National Archives the Library of  Congress the Chic